\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Representation of a finite-population Markov chain describing the stochastic evolution of two competing phenotypes A (yellow) and B (green). Each state represents a population composition, with transitions between neighboring states governed by the probabilities \(T_{i,j}\). The intermediate states are transient, allowing fluctuations in phenotype frequencies, whereas the boundary states \(p=0\) and \(p=1\) are absorbing, corresponding to the fixation of one phenotype and the extinction of the other, inspired by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {nowakEvolutionaryDynamicsExploring2006}.}}{8}{figure.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Schematic representation of the Moran process. At each step, in a finite population of $N$ individuals, an individual is selected for reproduction (duplication) with a probability proportional to its fitness (upper arrow), while another is chosen at random to die (lower arrow). The offspring of the first replaces the second, keeping the population size constant. This iterative process allows observation of how phenotypes spread or go extinct over time inspired by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {nowakEvolutionaryDynamicsExploring2006}.}}{12}{figure.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Three-dimensional fitness landscape showing fitness \(W\)as a function of the initial density of phenotype A \(P_A\) and predator density \(\eta _d\), illustrating key topological features such as local peaks, valleys, and saddle points inspired by \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {nowakEvolutionaryDynamicsExploring2006} .}}{13}{figure.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Deterministic replicator dynamics of the sexually selected phenotype A under five evolutionary regimes: (I) A dominance, (II) B dominance, (III) stable coexistence, (IV) bistability, and (V) neutrality. Each curve represents a different initial phenotype frequency ($p_0$), while predation intensity ($\eta _d$) and reproductive advantage ($r_0$) remain fixed within each scenario. The horizontal axis represents simulation steps in arbitrary time units, and the vertical axis represents the proportion of phenotype A. The corresponding frequencies maximizing mean population fitness are $p_{\max }\approx 0.7105$, $0$, $0.25$, and $1$ for regimes I--IV, respectively. In the neutral regime (V), mean fitness is constant for all population compositions. These fitness maxima do not necessarily coincide with the stable evolutionary equilibria.}}{26}{figure.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Deterministic replicator dynamics of the sexually selected phenotype A under two evolutionary regimes: (I) dominance of phenotype A at \((\eta _d=0)\) and (II) stable coexistence at \((\eta _d=1)\). Each curve represents a different initial proportion of phenotype A (\(p_0)\), while predation intensity \(\eta _d)\) remains fixed within each scenario. The horizontal axis represents simulation steps in arbitrary time units, whereas the vertical axis represents the proportion of phenotype A in the population, \(p\). In both regimes, trajectories with different initial proportions converge toward the same equilibrium, determined by the corresponding predation intensity.}}{27}{figure.5}%
